<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One, Two, Three Bananas! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> One, Two, Three Bananas!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify key details in a story. Reading: Connect text content to personal experiences or related activities. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify key shopping words in a story and highlight or list them (e.g., "apples" and "bananas"). Can connect a story about shopping to personal experience by listing familiar items (e.g., "I buy apples, too."). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words, such as blending /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ sounds to form the word "sun" (one syllable) and blending /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ sounds to form the word "flower"(two syllables) when the words have been previously encountered.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of fruits introduced in Lesson 1 (e.g., apple, banana, pineapple, orange). The teacher holds up a flashcard and asks, "What is this?" and "How many do you see?" Students respond chorally or individually (e.g., "Banana!" "Three bananas!"). The teacher then quickly reviews phonemic blending for previously encountered numbers or shopping words. For example, the teacher says sounds /s/ /ɪ/ /k/ /s/ slowly and asks students to blend them into a word: "What word is it?" (Six!).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite expressions for requesting items and expressing quantity. For example, holding up a picture of three apples, the teacher says clearly: "I want three apples, please." The teacher emphasizes the /p/ sound in "please" and "apple" and the /b/ sound in "banana" when presenting those words. The teacher demonstrates blending sounds for words like "money" (/m/ /ʌ/ /n/ /i/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of quantity and vocabulary before reading.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing two oranges) "Is this one orange or two oranges?" (Expected answer: "Two oranges!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of money) "Is this a banana or money?" (Expected answer: "Money!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story or a simple narrative text titled "My Shopping Trip." The story features a child going to a market/store and buying various fruits, explicitly using target vocabulary (apple, banana, pineapple, orange, money, cart) and grammar (Number + noun, Present simple, numbers). The text is simple and repetitive, designed for A1.1 readers. Example sentences: "I go to the store. I see big red apples. I want three apples, please. I put them in my basket. I see yellow bananas. I want two bananas, please."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend short, simple narrative texts on familiar topics to identify key details and main ideas. Reading: Understand and identify numbers and quantities in written text. Phonemic Awareness: Practice blending sounds within words heard in the story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud first. Students listen and follow along in their books. Then, the teacher asks, "Who goes shopping?" (The child). "What does the child buy?" (Fruits).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading (Matching):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of fruits mentioned in the story and corresponding numbers (e.g., three apples, two bananas). They read sentences from the story (e.g., "I want three apples.") and draw a line to match the sentence to the correct picture.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> After reading, the teacher highlights a word from the story, e.g., "banana." The teacher says the sounds slowly /b/ /ə/ /n/ /ɑː/ /n/ /ə/, and asks students to repeat and blend the sounds. Repeat for "apple" or "money."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading shopping-related vocabulary (nouns: pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, store, basket, price, cashier; verbs: buy, pick, carry, cost, need, ask, shop) and grammar (Number + noun, Present simple: "I want...", "I buy..."). Practicing polite expressions (e.g., "Can I have... please?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Cloze Activity:</b> Students receive a simplified version of the "My Shopping Trip" story with key shopping words (e.g., apple, banana, money, buy, store, numbers) removed. They read the text and choose the correct word from a word bank to fill in the blanks. Example: "I go to the ______ (store/park). I want ______ (one/ten) apples."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – Read and Draw:</b> Students get a worksheet with short sentences like, "I want three oranges." or "I see five pineapples." Below each sentence, they read and draw the correct number of the specified fruit. This reinforces reading comprehension of numbers and nouns.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Read and Circle:</b> In pairs, students receive a short, illustrated paragraph about someone going shopping. One student reads a sentence aloud (e.g., "My mom wants four fresh bananas."). The other student listens and circles the number and the fruit in their own copy of the text. They then switch roles. This encourages careful reading and identification of key details in context, including pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Reading Activity - "My Shopping List." Students work individually with a personalized "shopping list" (a simple text with illustrations) that they need to "read" and "collect" items for. The lists use vocabulary like "apple, banana, pineapple, orange" and numbers "one, two, three, four, five, ten."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a small, illustrated shopping list (e.g., "I need two apples.", "I want one banana.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places large flashcards or pictures of fruits with numbers (e.g., a picture of 3 apples, a picture of 5 bananas) around the classroom acting as "market stalls."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each item on their list. Once they read an item, they "go" to the corresponding "stall" (picture) and identify if the quantity matches their list. For example, if their list says "I need three apples," they find the picture of three apples.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they find each item, they tick it off their list and say aloud "I found three apples!" or "I buy two bananas." (using polite expressions where applicable, "Can I have two bananas, please?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task focuses on students actively reading their own list and matching text details to visual information in a dynamic way.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Shopping List" activity and an individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Observation:</b> The teacher circulates and observes students as they read their shopping lists and locate items. The teacher checks if they can correctly identify words and numbers from their list.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a small paper. They are asked to:
                            <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read the sentence: "I like to buy..." and complete it by drawing one fruit they buy.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Underneath the drawing, they write the name of the fruit (e.g., "apple").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional: Teacher says "/m/ /ʌ/ /n/ /i/" and students write the word 'money' on the back).</li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify key shopping words (e.g., "apples", "bananas") in the written text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can connect the written quantity (numbers) to visual representations.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can blend spoken phonemes to form a familiar one or two-syllable word.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in drawing and writing the name of the fruit.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I read my shopping list carefully?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word I learned to read today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What fruit did you like reading about today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you like to buy when you go to the store with your family?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading strategies worked best for identifying key details in the story?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to connect the story to their personal experiences effectively?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for supporting students with blending phonemes in next lessons?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What vocabulary words need more reinforcement?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson prepares students for actively speaking and interacting about shopping in Lesson 3. The vocabulary and grammar structures practiced today will be used to create simple dialogues and requests. The next lesson will transition from reading comprehension to oral production, asking and answering questions about shopping lists and preferences, using the same "Let's Go Shopping!" scenario and fruit vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>